# Where wall-clock goes in an RLVR training step.
# Numbers MEASURED on codecontests_rlvr_olmo31_32b_smoke10 (Olmo-3.1-32B,
# 8xH200, batch 8 x group 8 = 64 rollouts/step, response_length 8192).
# Verifier timings re-measured locally by replaying all 640 completions.
# Render: d2 docs/rlvr_wallclock.d2 docs/rlvr_wallclock.svg

direction: down

title: |md
  # RLVR step: 171 s train-only, measured
  Olmo-3.1-32B / 8xH200 / 64 rollouts per step. **MATH runs 48 s/step** on a
  7B with `response_length: 2000` — the gap is model size x tokens generated.
| {near: top-center}

loop: "for step in range(cfg.steps)   —   infra/train.py:100" {
  style.stroke-dash: 3

  sync: |md
    ### 1. sync_sampler
    `train.py:101`
    LoRA weights -> vLLM
    **not separately measured**
  |

  rollout: "2. env.rollout()   —   train.py:102" {
    style.fill: "#eef4fa"

    gen: |md
      ### generation (vLLM)
      `envs/base.py:149` `policy.predict`
      64 samples, TP=8

      median **2663 tok**, p90 **8192**
      **24% hit the 8192 cap**
      72% of text is prose before
      the code block

      **the dominant cost**
    |

    verify: |md
      ### verifier (subprocess)
      `envs/base.py:162` pool ->
      `tasks/codecontests.py:143`
      `run_stdin_tests`

      607/640 finish, median **0.415 s**
      20/640 (3.2%) hit the **30 s** timeout

      wallclock **~30 s/step (18%)**
      = the single slowest program
    |

    dump: |md
      ### on_rollout
      `train.py:103` -> `run_rlvr.py`
      writes rollouts/step-N.jsonl
      **negligible**
    |
  }

  pack: |md
    ### 3. grpo_pack
    `train.py:110`
    CPU only — **negligible**
  |

  kl: |md
    ### 4. apply_kl_penalty
    `train.py:122` -> `rl/kl.py:54`
    `backend.ref_logprobs(datums)`

    **a FULL extra forward pass**
    over every datum on the frozen
    base, every step, to serve
    `kl_coef: 0.02`

    **UNMEASURED** — no phase timer
  |

  fb: |md
    ### 5. forward_backward + optim
    `train.py:133-135`
    micro_batch 64, n_datums ~48
    -> 1 call per epoch

    **UNMEASURED**
  |

  ev: |md
    ### 6. evaluate  (only if step % eval_every == 0)
    `train.py:136` -> `train.py:87`
    greedy rollout on the TEST split + the same verifier

    measured **4.7 s per eval sample**
    at `eval_n: 256` that is **~20 min per eval**
    at `eval_every: 5` over 100 steps -> **6.7 h = 58% of the run**

    ### this is redundant
    accuracy is supposed to come from the OFFLINE
    CodeContests-O pass over the dumped rollouts
    (`tasks/codecontests.py` module docstring)
  | {style.fill: "#fdecea"; style.stroke: "#c0392b"}

  save: |md
    ### 7. save
    `train.py:148` if `save_every`
    `train.py:155` `save("final")` — **always**, 3.1 GB
  |

  sync -> rollout -> pack -> kl -> fb -> ev -> save
}

known: |||md
  ## What is measured vs not

  | phase | per step | source |
  |---|---|---|
  | verifier | **30 s** (18%) | replayed 640 completions locally |
  | eval (n=32) | **151 s** | step_seconds delta on eval steps |
  | one-off init | **403 s** | step 0 minus mean eval step |
  | generation + KL + fwd/bwd | **~141 s** | **residual — not attributed** |

  `train.py:150` records only `step_seconds`. Splitting the residual needs
  phase timers around each call above (~10 lines).
||| {style.fill: "#f4f6f8"}

levers: |||md
  ## Levers, 100 steps at \$36.72/h

  | change | run time | cost |
  |---|---|---|
  | as configured (`eval_n 256`, `eval_every 5`) | 11.6 h | **\$425** |
  | drop in-run eval (use the offline pass) | 4.9 h | **\$179** |
  | + `timeout_seconds` 30 -> 5 | 4.2 h | **\$154** |
  | + `response_length` 8192 -> 2048 | not yet measured | — |

  Concurrency is NOT a lever: 8 vs 64 verifier workers measured 30.1 s vs
  30.2 s, because wallclock is the slowest single program, not throughput.
||| {style.fill: "#eaf5ea"}

bug: |md
  ## Bug found while measuring

  `backend/verl.py:263`
  ```
  raw_reason = str(out.stop_reason or "stop")
  ```
  vLLM puts "stop"/"length" in **`finish_reason`**; `stop_reason` is the
  matched stop STRING and is `None` for both natural EOS and truncation.
  So `or "stop"` labels **everything** "stop".

  All 640 completions report `stop` while **24% sit exactly at the 8192
  cap** — those are truncated, usually mid-program, and are trained on as
  if they finished. It also hides truncation from `fidelity_ok()`
  (`backend/base.py:70`).
| {style.fill: "#fdecea"; style.stroke: "#c0392b"}

loop -> known: {style.stroke-dash: 2}
known -> levers: {style.stroke-dash: 2}
levers -> bug: {style.stroke-dash: 2}
